<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serpent Column</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">479 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Serpent Column is a bronze column of three intertwined snakes that once supported a golden tripod dedicated at Delphi by the Greek states that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> in 479 BCE. Its coils bear the names of 31 allied states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where its damaged remains still stand in the Hippodrome, today's Sultanahmet Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serpent-column.jpg" alt="Serpent Column" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Serpent_Column.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
